id: pm-4zpq71
title: "pm config set on a schema-invalid settings.json writes the fallback defaults as the whole file, deleting unrelated settings"
description: "When `settings.json` is valid JSON but fails schema validation (for example an unknown value in a validated enum such as `context.next_ranking_policy`, `validation.parent_reference` or `item_format`), `readSettingsWithMetadata` returns `buildFallbackSettingsReadResult(\"settings_read_invalid_schema\")`. That result is the full default settings with no persistence snapshot. A following `pm config project set <key> <value>` then writes those defaults as the complete document, silently removing every unrelated stored setting. The existing write guard refuses only malformed JSON (`settings_read_invalid_json`), not schema-invalid JSON.\n\npm-x2aplf fixed the same overwrite for valid sparse files (persist only the changed key). Its acceptance criteria do not cover the invalid-schema read path. CodeRabbit found this on PR #1638: the new `context.next_ranking_policy` enum adds one more way to reach it, but the behaviour predates that change and applies to every validated setting."
type: Issue
status: closed
priority: 1
tags[4]: config,data-integrity,review,settings
created_at: "2026-10-31T16:19:52.261Z"
updated_at: "2026-11-01T18:08:29.719Z"
closed_at: "2026-11-01T12:21:40.348Z"
completed_at: "2026-11-01T12:21:40.348Z"
author: "harness:claude-code"
estimated_minutes: 90
acceptance_criteria: "A config write against a settings.json that fails schema validation is refused with a code naming the invalid field (no write), or preserves the original document and changes only the requested key; a regression test seeds a schema-invalid settings.json with unrelated keys, runs pm config project set, and asserts the unrelated keys survive or the write is refused; the same holds for SDK config writers; An explicit repair to the default value takes effect: pm config <scope> set <dotted.key> <default> repairs the stored field with changed: true and keeps unrelated keys, SDK callers pass writeSettings options.assignedPaths, and no pm config setter reports a silent changed: false over an unreadable document"
parent: pm-o2kc
risk: high
confidence: high
resolution: "Fallback settings carry a non-enumerable origin marker (kept across cached reads); writeSettings applies only the defaults-to-proposal delta onto the locked stored document and refuses settings_write_invalid_existing_schema with context.field naming the first invalid dotted path when the result is still invalid; validateSettings now reports that path; automatic writers (mutation preflight item_format pin, telemetry installation id, first-run consent) skip via the new public isSettingsReadFallback helper. Documented in docs/CONFIGURATION.md."
expected_result: A config or SDK write against a schema-invalid settings.json is refused with a field-naming code or changes only the requested key; unrelated keys always survive.
actual_result: pm config project set locks.ttl_seconds on a schema-invalid file refuses with settings_write_invalid_existing_schema naming context.next_ranking_policy and leaves bytes and history untouched; setting context.next_ranking_policy weighted repairs the file and keeps id_prefix and extension sections; automatic writers leave the bytes unchanged; full suite and coverage pass.
dependencies[1]{id,kind,created_at,author,source_kind,author_source}:
  pm-x2aplf,related,"2026-10-31T16:19:52.261Z","harness:claude-code","cli:create:dep",detected
comments[7]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-11-01T11:02:51.594Z","harness:claude-code","Implementation: a default fallback returned for an unreadable settings.json (warnings settings_read_invalid_schema, settings_read_fs_error, settings_read_merge_failed) now carries a non-enumerable fallback-origin marker. cloneSettingsReadResult copies it, so cached reads keep it. writeSettings without a persist snapshot checks the marker. Under the workspace-history lock it applies only the delta from the canonical defaults to the caller's proposal onto the stored document it re-reads. The result is validated. If it is still invalid, the write refuses with settings_write_invalid_existing_schema (exit 2): context.field names the first invalid dotted path, original_code is the read warning, and nothing is written and no history is appended. If it validates, it is written with every unrelated stored key kept, including extension-owned sections. The settings validator now reports the failing path: Failure carries path segments through vObject, vArray and vRecordOf, and validateSettings returns { success: false, path }. Chosen behaviour: AC option 2 (preserve and change only the requested key) combined with option 1's refusal when the result would stay invalid. pm config project set context.next_ranking_policy weighted can therefore repair the file, while an unrelated set refuses with the field to fix. Tests: tests/unit/core/store/settings-fallback-write.spec.ts, 10 cases including the CLI regression. All 195 store tests pass.\n"
  "2026-11-01T11:26:14.659Z","harness:claude-code","Follow-up from the full suite: three AUTOMATIC writers also acted on the defaults fallback. They are the mutation preflight item_format pin (src/cli/migration-gates.ts), the telemetry installation-id write (src/core/telemetry/runtime.ts ensureInstallationId) and the first-run consent prompt (src/core/telemetry/consent.ts). Before this change, each of them silently replaced an unreadable settings.json with defaults. cli-main-errors.spec even asserted that a {version:1} document was rewritten with item_format toon. With the new refusal, the pin would have blocked every mutation command on a tracker with schema-invalid settings. New public helper isSettingsReadFallback(settings), exported from the SDK runtime primitives: the three automatic writers now skip their write when settings are a fallback. The command runs on the defaults with the existing warning, telemetry captures nothing until the global settings are readable, and the consent prompt is not shown because its answer could not be recorded. User-requested writes (config, schema, package install, init on an existing tracker) keep the field-naming refusal. Tests: the cli-main-errors expectation now asserts the document is byte-identical, and telemetry-consent.spec and telemetry-runtime.spec each gained an unreadable-global-settings case.\n"
  "2026-11-01T14:03:49.000Z","harness:claude-code","PR 1648 review follow-up (owner verification comment): an explicit repair to the DEFAULT value could not repair an invalid stored field, because the defaults-to-proposed delta is empty when the assigned value equals the default; pm config set even reported changed: false with no write. Fix: writeSettings(pmRoot, settings, op, { assignedPaths }) applies explicitly assigned dotted paths over an unreadable document even when equal to the default (readable documents ignore the option; unsafe segments refuse with settings_write_assigned_path_invalid). pm config <scope> set <dotted.key> passes its path and reports changed: true; every other pm config setter now writes over a fallback so it returns the typed settings_write_invalid_existing_schema refusal instead of a silent changed: false. Snapshot-only SDK repairs to the default still refuse truthfully with the field named. Covered in tests/unit/core/store/settings-fallback-write.spec.ts (default-valued and non-default repairs, cached reads, unchanged history on refusal, CLI default repair and two setter no-op refusals)."
  "2026-11-01T15:40:55.396Z","harness:claude-code","PR 1648 review follow-up (CodeRabbit). Every specialized pm config setter now passes the settings paths it assigns (for example sprint_release_format_policy assigns validation.sprint_release_format, governance policy setters assign governance.preset plus their field, the context setter assigns each provided option, item_format assigns item_format), so setting a default value repairs that field through any setter, not only pm config set <dotted.key>. A setter that keeps the default of a different invalid field still returns the typed refusal. After a successful write from a fallback object, its next write measures the change from the document it last wrote instead of from the defaults, so a peer's later edit to a field this object already wrote is kept. The refusal's next steps no longer promise an assigned-path repair for every field: a record key containing a dot cannot be addressed that way, so the first step is to fix or remove the field in settings.json, with pm config named only for configurable settings. Ordering note: gate_evidence and escape_class were recorded after close and release because the defect-evidence gate first failed on the hosted run; they are follow-up records, the append-only history is unchanged."
  "2026-11-01T17:05:14.905Z","harness:claude-code","PR 1648 second review round (CodeRabbit). The mutation preflight now skips the automatic pre-mutation item-format migration on a defaults fallback, not only the item_format pin: the fallback's format and schema can differ from the stored ones (for example a custom status mapping), and the migration could rewrite items under the wrong schema and remove the originals. cli-main-errors asserts the migration path never runs and fails without the guard. Assigned paths now also cover keys that canonical serialization omits: a core-section path whose parent the proposed settings carry but whose value they omit (a default-valued optional key such as governance.require_close_reason, or a cleared key such as governance.create_default_type) is removed from the stored document, so pm config set governance_require_close_reason enabled repairs a stored 'yes' and pm config set governance_create_default_type with an empty value clears an invalid value. Extension-owned sections are never cleared this way. The SDK surface acknowledgement now states the fallback write behavior change instead of claiming unchanged behavior."
  "2026-11-01T17:31:24.291Z","harness:claude-code","PR 1648 Codex review (gpt-6.1-sol, xhigh): a specialized pm config setter that repaired an unreadable document to a default value still reported changed: false, because its comparison ran against the fallback defaults. The config context now records a fallback repair write and the set dispatcher reports changed: true; the context setter reports it directly. CLI specs assert changed: true for the policy, context, require_close_reason and create_default_type repairs, and fail without the fix."
  "2026-11-01T18:08:29.719Z","harness:claude-code","PR 1648 Codex review, second pass: pm config set item_format over a schema-invalid document migrated item files (deleting the Markdown originals) before the settings write refused, so the refusal claimed nothing changed. Over a fallback, the settings are now written first with item_format assigned, so a still-invalid document refuses before any item file is touched, and the migration then runs with the re-read repaired document's own schema and type folders instead of the fallback defaults; an interrupted migration is converged by the next mutation's preflight sync. Readable documents keep migrate-then-write. In-process and CLI specs cover the refusal (Markdown item intact) and the repair (item migrated, changed: true)."
